WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.050
So here's a proverbial black box,
and let's stipulate for the moment

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:07.310
that I have no idea how hashing works,
but I do know that this box can do it.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.230
So how do I think about this process?

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:13.100
Well generally speaking, there's
going to be some input to this box.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.230
Ultimately, I want to get
some output from that box.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:20.750
And what this box really represents
is, in fact, a hash function.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.660
You can think of this as a
device like some kind of machine;

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.570
you can think of it like a
program, some piece of software;

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:30.160
or you can even think about it as a
mathematical function that operates

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.360
simply on numbers coming in as input.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.210
In fact, if you're
mathematically inclined,

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.140
though we won't use this syntax often,
you can think of that hash function

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:42.850
as being represented by f, you can think
of the input as being represented by x,

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:47.278
and you can think of the output of
this process as being so-called f of x.

00:00:47.278 --> 00:00:49.570
If you're not familiar with
that notation, that's fine,

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:53.350
but this is directly connected
hashing to basic mathematics

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.480
as well that you might
encounter before long.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.530
But what we care about is passing
into this black box a password

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:03.040
and getting out a hash, and
then storing that hash and not

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.270
the password in our database or text
file of usernames and passwords.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.200
So how might we go about doing this?

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.360
Well, if I were to provide apple
as an input to this hash function,

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.510
let's think about the simplest
hash function possible

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:21.700
that doesn't output apple, but
some representation of apple

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.925
that I can eventually
store in that database.

00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:27.050
So I'm going to propose very simply
that maybe the a simplest hash function

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.930
we can come up with-- and indeed,
if you've studied computer science

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.600
or taken CS50 itself,
you might recall that we

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.900
can hash our inputs unlike
specific letters therein.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.080
So apple starts with
A. So you know what?

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.090
A is the first letter
of the English alphabet.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.070
So I'm going to create
a hash function here

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:47.960
pictorially that outputs one whenever
the input happens to start with an A,

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.130
as does apple.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.590
Meanwhile, if we pass
in banana, I'm going

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.140
to have this hash function
output 2 because B

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.270
is the letter of the English alphabet.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:01.100
And dot-dot-dot, we might get to cherry
or other passwords as well that might

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.840
output 3 and beyond.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.470
And you could imagine doing this for
all letters of the English alphabet.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.470
Now unfortunately, this
isn't the best hash function

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.240
because it's fairly simplistic.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.780
And in fact, I can quickly think
of some other fruits like avocados

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.493
that also start with A and that
would give me the same hash value.

00:02:18.493 --> 00:02:20.660
And that's actually a
characteristic we'll come back

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.430
to whereby when you hash values,
there can actually be ambiguities,

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:30.180
potentially, whereby two inputs
might actually have the same output,

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.400
and we'll consider eventually what
the implications of that might be.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.240
But for now, I dare say that's
a little too simplistic.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.170
And what might be better
than outputting 1 or 2 or 3

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:43.920
is a little something more cryptic,
because that's just too helpful.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.070
That's too much of a hint.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.880
If I see that your hash
value is 1, I at least

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.930
know that your password now clearly
starts with an A, which means at best,

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.170
I can do 1/26th the amount of work
to figure out what it actually is.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:00.210
So we want these hashes generally to
be a little weird-looking and really

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.040
unguessable and not
leak any information.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.560
So for instance, a very common older
hash function for apple might actually

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:15.180
output this-- ..ekWXa83dhiA with some
mixed uppercase and lowercase letters

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:15.780
therein.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.780
Now it looks weird, you probably can't
and shouldn't see any kind of pattern

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:20.280
in there.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:24.090
There is a fancy math formula
that took as input apple

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:28.020
and outputted as its hash
value that string of text

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.770
there, but in and of itself, it
doesn't really leak any information

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.870
like the number 1 or 2 or 3 would.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.520
So we've already made an improvement.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.110
Banana, meanwhile, would look like this.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.780
And cherry, meanwhile,
would look like that.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.840
So notice that these values
are indeed quite different.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.920
So using this better hash function,
I claim, that doesn't just

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.660
look at the first
letter of the alphabet,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.810
but looks at maybe all of
the letters in the input--

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:55.980
C-H-E-R-R-Y in this case, we can
probably come up with something more

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.530
interesting, more
cryptic-looking, if you will,

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.150
like the values that we've just seen.
